  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 137 AM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge remains centered near the Arizona/New Mexico\nborder early this morning, leaving most of Colorado in weak\nwester/northwesterly flow aloft. This ridge is expected to slowly\nshift eastward over the next several days with few other synoptic\nscale features of note. \n\nThe plume of better monsoon moisture will remain well off to the\nwest and northwest today. Modest mid-level moisture combined with\ndeep mixing will lead to widely scattered showers and\nthunderstorms this afternoon, though most of the convection should\nbe weak given only meager instability. Most forecast soundings\nshow inverted-V profiles across most of the I-25 corridor, and\ngusty outflow winds will be the main hazard this afternoon and\nevening. Meanwhile, it'll be another hot day today as 700mb\ntemperatures climb back to 16-18C. High temperatures should reach\nthe low to mid 90s across most of the lower elevations.\n\nSaturday features more of the same, with the flow aloft backing to\nthe west-southwest. Models generally try to increase moisture\naloft a bit, with precipitable water values settling near an inch\nacross most of the I-25 corridor, with more anomalous PW across\nnorthern/northwestern Colorado. Guidance generally tries to\ndevelop a more organized band of showers and thunderstorms close\nto the Wyoming border, associated with a weak mid-level shortwave\nand cold front. The ECMWF remains a bit more bullish with this\npotential compared to the rest of tonight's guidance, though even\nthe more aggressive solutions don't have much in the way of\nrainfall outside of the northern mountains/North Park.\n\nMoisture increases a little bit more on Sunday, with another weak\nshortwave racing through the southwesterly flow aloft. Surface\nmoisture will remain a limiting factor with dew points in the 40s\nleading to meager CAPE values across most of the forecast area.\nAnother, perhaps better defined shortwave moves through on Monday\nand will likely provide our best chance of rainfall, though sfc\nmoisture/instability will again be a bit of a question mark.\n\nBy mid-week, most of the remaining moisture aloft sinks off to the\nsouth, meaning that near/above normal temperatures and generally\ndry conditions are forecast through the rest of the period.",
